<h1>What is confirmed, and what is not</h1>
<p class="lede">This project runs on a division of labour: a panel argues, a chair synthesizes, and the author ratifies. Ratification is the one step performed by a person standing outside the machine. This page states, plainly, what the author has confirmed and what he has not — so no reader has to reconstruct it from the record.</p>

<p class="companion-note">Plain-language key. <strong>Ratified</strong> = the author, the one human check, has confirmed it item-by-item. <strong>Author-authorized default (S4k)</strong> = a low-stakes, subtractive, or labeling-only item the author pre-approved as a batch ("apply") without ruling on each — confirmed by his authorization, not by line-by-line review. <strong>Provisional-pending-author</strong> = produced by the panel and chair (which are, on the project's own admission, one underlying model in many roles) under a delegation and not yet reviewed by the author. As of Session 4k the standing tower of provisional items is resolved; the moratorium (Rule 25) freezes new delegations.</p>

<h2>The standing count</h2>
<p>As of <strong>Session 4k</strong>: the author returned — not with a fourth delegation, but to <strong>rule</strong>. The three-storey provisional tower built across Sessions 4h, 4i, and 4j (booked as costs C-027, C-032, C-035) is <strong>resolved</strong>. He read the forum's decision set, answered twelve load-bearing questions himself (each shown to him as multiple choice with the chair's recommendation), authorized a named residue of low-stakes defaults, and directed completion of the build. The honest limit sits beside this and does not move: ratification is <strong>one human check on an otherwise one-model system</strong>, "the author judged it complete" is a single self-reported indicator — not evidence of health — and there remains <strong>zero external confirmation of anything</strong>, which by the author's own S4k concession will not change in this build.</p>

